  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Webull Alternative",
    "body": "Choosing an alternative to Webull begins with checking the exact fees tied to the assets you trade most often. While Webull and all five alternatives list $0 online commissions for US stocks and ETFs, their fees diverge sharply when you trade equity options, index options, mutual funds or cryptocurrency.\n\nFirst, calculate your options contract volume. Webull charges $0 broker commissions on standard equity options trades, but charges a $0.50 per contract fee on certain index options. Firstrade charges $0 in broker commissions and $0 in options contract fees on equity options. In contrast, Charles Schwab charges $0.65 per contract, while Interactive Brokers charges between $0.15 and $0.65 per contract on IBKR Pro tiered schedules or a fixed $0.65 per contract. tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open an equity option, but caps that commission at $10 per leg and charges $0 to close the position. Trading 100 contracts on a single leg costs $0 at Webull, $0 at Firstrade, $65 at Charles Schwab ($0.65 multiplied by 100), and $10 at tastytrade due to its $10 per-leg cap.\n\nSecond, evaluate index options pricing. Webull charges $0.50 per contract on certain index options. An investor trading 20 of these index contracts at Webull incurs a $10 contract charge ($0.50 multiplied by 20). At tastytrade, broad-based index options cost $1 per contract to open and $0 to close without the $10 leg cap, meaning 20 index contracts cost $20 to open.\n\nThird, review order assistance and non-digital channels. If you require telephone or representative support to place orders, other brokers publish distinct service fees. Charles Schwab adds a $25 service charge for broker-assisted trades. Firstrade charges $19.95 for broker-assisted stock and ETF trades, and $19.95 plus $0.50 per contract for broker-assisted options orders.\n\nFourth, compare margin borrowing costs if you trade on leverage. Webull publishes pass-through regulatory fees and transaction terms, but margin borrowers should examine baseline interest schedules. Fidelity publishes a base margin rate of 10.825% effective since September 18, 2026, which steps down to 10.325% on balances between $250,000 and $499,999. Interactive Brokers offers tiered and fixed schedules on its Pro tier.\n\nFifth, consider account mobility fees. Webull lists a $75 fee per outgoing stock transfer when moving shares to another brokerage. If you plan to transfer existing assets, verify whether the receiving broker covers transfer charges or if you will absorb the $75 fee.\n\nReview this short checklist before selecting a broker:\n\n- How many standard equity options contracts do you open and close each month?\n- Do you trade broad-based index options that carry separate per-contract fees?\n- Do you require broker-assisted phone orders, and what is the fee per trade?\n- Does the broker charge an outgoing account transfer fee if you move assets later?\n- Are mutual funds part of your portfolio, and does the broker charge transaction fees for them?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Charles Schwab Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Charles Schwab charges $0 for online equity trade commissions on listed stocks and ETFs. For options trades, Charles Schwab charges a $0 base commission plus $0.65 per options contract. Charles Schwab enforces no account minimums and no trade minimums to open an account or place an order.\n\nFor orders placed with staff support, Charles Schwab adds a $25 service charge for broker-assisted trades. For cryptocurrency transactions, Charles Schwab charges 0.75% of trade value per buy or sell, with a minimum fee of $0.01 per order.\n\nA worked options cost example shows how Charles Schwab compares with Webull. An investor trading 100 standard equity options contracts pays $65 at Charles Schwab (100 multiplied by $0.65), while Webull charges $0 in commissions for standard equity options. On an order of 10 options contracts, Charles Schwab charges $6.50 (10 multiplied by $0.65), compared to $0 at Webull. For crypto transactions, buying $1,000 in cryptocurrency at Charles Schwab costs $7.50 in trade value fees ($1,000 multiplied by 0.0075).\n\nCharles Schwab fits investors who want $0 stock commissions and published options pricing of $0.65 per contract. Against Webull, Charles Schwab falls short for high-volume equity options traders who prefer Webull's $0 per-contract commission structure, and Charles Schwab applies a $25 fee whenever an investor requires broker phone assistance."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Fidelity Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Fidelity offers $0 commissions for online US stock, ETF and option trades, though option contract fees apply to option orders. \n\nFidelity publishes a margin interest schedule built on a base rate of 10.825%, which took effect on September 18, 2026. Fidelity reduces this margin borrowing rate as balance tiers increase. For example, accounts carrying a debit balance from $250,000 to $499,999 receive a 10.325% margin interest rate.\n\nOn equity trades, both Fidelity and Webull charge $0 in commissions for online US stocks and ETFs. For options, Webull lists $0 commissions on standard equity options alongside its $0.50 index options fee, while Fidelity states that option contract fees apply to options orders. On a margin loan balance of $300,000, Fidelity charges 10.325% annually, which amounts to $30,975 in yearly borrowing interest ($300,000 multiplied by 0.10325).\n\nFidelity fits investors seeking $0 commissions on US stocks and ETFs who want transparent margin borrowing schedules with balance tiers starting from a 10.825% base rate. Against Webull, Fidelity falls short on published per-contract options clarity on its primary rate overview, where it notes that option contract fees apply rather than displaying a single zero-dollar options rate."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interactive Brokers Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Interactive Brokers provides two distinct service tiers: IBKR Lite and IBKR Pro. IBKR Lite offers $0 commissions on US exchange-listed stocks and ETFs for US residents. IBKR Pro uses tiered or fixed commission schedules. For stocks and ETFs, IBKR Pro tiered pricing runs from $0.0005 to $0.0035 per share, while fixed pricing costs $0.005 per share. For options, IBKR Pro tiered pricing ranges from $0.15 to $0.65 per contract, while fixed options pricing costs $0.65 per contract. Interactive Brokers publishes no account minimums and no hidden fees on its pricing overview.\n\nConsider an options trade of 10 contracts. On Webull, trading 10 standard equity options contracts costs $0 in broker commissions. On Interactive Brokers using IBKR Pro fixed pricing, 10 options contracts cost $6.50 (10 multiplied by $0.65). On IBKR Pro tiered pricing at the lowest $0.15 rate, those 10 contracts cost $1.50 (10 multiplied by $0.15). For stock trades, buying 1,000 shares of a US stock costs $0 on Webull and $0 on IBKR Lite, while costing $5 on IBKR Pro fixed ($0.005 multiplied by 1,000).\n\nInteractive Brokers fits active traders who want the flexibility of selecting either $0 Lite trading or Pro tiered execution pricing. Against Webull, Interactive Brokers falls short for retail traders who want simple $0 options execution, as IBKR Pro charges up to $0.65 per contract and fixed stock trading costs $0.005 per share."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Firstrade Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Firstrade provides commission-free trading across multiple retail investment categories. Firstrade charges $0 commission for stocks, ETFs and options, with a $0 options contract fee. In addition, mutual funds are free to trade at Firstrade, including load, no-load and no-transaction-fee (NTF) mutual funds.\n\nFor investors who utilize telephone assistance, Firstrade charges $19.95 for broker-assisted stock and ETF orders. Broker-assisted options trades cost $19.95 plus $0.50 per contract.\n\nWorked cost examples show parity on self-directed orders and clear charges on phone orders. Placing an online order for 100 equity options contracts costs $0 at Firstrade ($0 commission and $0 contract fee) and $0 at Webull. If an investor calls a broker to place that same 100-contract options order at Firstrade, the cost is $19.95 plus $50 (100 multiplied by $0.50), totaling $69.95. Placing a broker-assisted stock order at Firstrade costs $19.95, compared to $0 for an online trade.\n\nFirstrade fits cost-conscious options traders and fund investors who want $0 options contract charges alongside commission-free mutual fund trades. Against Webull, Firstrade falls short when investors require live broker phone support without paying the $19.95 trade service fee."
    },
    {
      "heading": "tastytrade Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "tastytrade provides options trading with a capped commission model. Stocks and ETFs cost $0 to open and $0 to close, with fractional share trading costing $0. For stock and ETF options, tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open, capped at $10 per leg, and $0 to close. Broad-based index options cost $1 per contract to open and $0 to close, with no leg cap. tastytrade charges no recurring platform subscriptions.\n\nFor cryptocurrency trading, tastytrade charges a 1% fee per purchase to open and close, which drops to 0.75% each way for Bitcoin (BTC) and Ethereum (ETH).\n\nThe capped fee model creates distinct arithmetic on larger single-leg options orders. Opening 50 contracts of an equity option on a single leg costs $10 at tastytrade because of the $10 per-leg cap (50 contracts at $1 would be $50, capped at $10), and closing costs $0. Webull charges $0 in commissions for standard equity options. However, opening 5 contracts at tastytrade costs $5 ($1 multiplied by 5). For broad-based index options, opening 20 contracts at tastytrade costs $20 (20 multiplied by $1), whereas Webull charges $0.50 per contract on certain index options, which equals $10 (20 multiplied by $0.50).\n\ntastytrade fits options traders who enter large-volume single-leg positions that benefit from the $10 opening commission cap and $0 closing fee. Against Webull, tastytrade falls short for smaller options orders under 10 contracts, where its $1 opening contract fee exceeds Webull's $0 commission on standard equity options."
