  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Webull Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Webull remains the closer match for retail investors who want zero-commission trading across US stocks, ETFs and standard equity options without paying per-contract options charges or monthly subscription fees. Webull lists no deposit minimums.\n\nWebull charges $0 on standard equity options contracts, whereas Charles Schwab charges $0.65 per contract, Interactive Brokers Pro charges up to $0.65 per contract, and tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open. For an investor trading 100 standard options contracts, Webull charges $0 in broker commissions, compared to $65 at Charles Schwab (100 multiplied by $0.65). While Webull applies pass-through regulatory fees such as the SEC fee of $0.0000206 times trade volume, it avoids recurring platform fees.\n\nAdditionally, Webull charges $0.50 per contract on certain index options. An investor trading 20 index contracts pays $10 at Webull ($0.50 multiplied by 20), whereas tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open without a cap, totaling $20.\n\n Firstrade offers commission-free mutual fund trading across load, no-load and NTF funds.\n- Webull fits poorly for investors who frequently transfer shares to outside brokerages, because Webull charges $75 per outgoing stock transfer.\n- Charles Schwab fits poorly for active options traders who want to avoid per-contract fees, because Schwab charges $0.65 per contract.\n- tastytrade fits poorly for traders opening small options positions of 1 to 5 contracts who want zero fees, because tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open.\n- Interactive Brokers Pro fits poorly for investors who want guaranteed zero-commission stock trades, because Pro charges $0.005 per share on fixed pricing.\n\nWhat Would Change Our Answer on Webull Alternatives\n\nPublished fee schedules determine the cost comparisons on this page. Several potential pricing changes would shift the competitive balance:\n\n- Webull introducing a per-contract fee on standard equity options would eliminate its cost advantage over Charles Schwab and Interactive Brokers.\n- Webull increasing or eliminating its $0.50 per contract fee on index options would alter comparisons against tastytrade's $1 index rate.\n- Webull adjusting its $75 outgoing stock transfer charge would change account portability math.\n- Charles Schwab lowering or eliminating its $0.65 options contract fee would make it directly competitive with Webull's zero-commission options structure.\n- Fidelity publishing a standardized per-contract options rate would allow direct dollar-for-dollar comparisons on equity options orders.\n- Firstrade adding per-contract options fees or trading fees on mutual funds would narrow its pricing distinction."
  },
